{
  // `strictNullChecks` and `noImplicitAny` are ON here, in the BASE config, and
  // there is deliberately no strict variant beside it.
  //
  // There used to be one — `tsconfig.strict.json`, added 2026-06-07. It was
  // never wired into a script or a CI step, so for two months it read as a
  // strictness policy while guarding nothing, and the 111 errors it would have
  // reported accumulated unseen. One of them was a live defect: a zero-price
  // add-on whose row had vanished answered 500 instead of 409, because `?.` in
  // the condition made the null case take the branch that dereferences it.
  //
  // A separate strict project would not have fixed that. It gives two sources
  // of truth: the editor and `ts-node` read THIS file and stay quiet, so new
  // code is written against the relaxed rules and only the extra CI step
  // objects — the ratchet slips by default. With the flags here, the editor,
  // the build, the test runner and CI all ask the same question.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "commonjs",
    "declaration": true,
    "removeComments": true,
    "emitDecoratorMetadata": true,
    "experimentalDecorators": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "target": "ES2021",
    "sourceMap": true,
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "baseUrl": "./",
    "incremental": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "strictBindCallApply": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": false,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": false,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "ignoreDeprecations": "5.0",
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["src/*"]
    }
  },
  // `ts-node` builds its program by FOLLOWING IMPORTS from the entry file and
  // ignores `include` unless told otherwise. Ambient declarations that nothing
  // imports are therefore invisible to it — `src/types/express.d.ts`, which
  // augments `Express.Request` with `correlationId`, is the one we have.
  //
  // Until `noImplicitAny` was turned on this cost nothing visible: under the
  // test runner `req['correlationId']` silently resolved to `any`, so the two
  // correlation middlewares were checked against a DIFFERENT program than the
  // one `npm run typecheck` compiles. That divergence is the same shape as the
  // gap this whole change closes, so it is fixed here rather than papered over
  // with a triple-slash reference in each affected file — which would have to
  // be remembered for every future declaration.
  //
  // The cost is real and measured, not waved away: every spec process loads the
  // whole `src` program, and the full suite went from 138s to 213s on a warm
  // local run. That is the price of the runner and the compiler agreeing.
  "ts-node": { "files": true },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "web",
    "web/**/*",
    "**/*.spec.ts",
    "**/*.test.ts"
  ]
}
